## Tutorials

You getNaNwhen JavaScript cannot calculate a number.

The type ofNaNisnumber.

This may look strange, butNaNbelongs to the JavaScript number type.

JavaScript tries to convert numeric strings to numbers in arithmetic operations.

A non-numeric string cannot be converted to a number.

You can use the JavaScript functionisNaN()to find out if a value is not a number.

NaNis the only JavaScript value that is not equal to itself.

To test forNaN, useisNaN().

If you useNaNin a mathematical operation, the result will also beNaN.

### Code Examples

```
let x = 100 / "Apple";
document.getElementById("demo").innerHTML = x;

---

let x = NaN;
document.getElementById("demo").innerHTML = typeof x;

---

